;
; ec20-ports.inf
;
; Binds the Microsoft inbox USB serial driver (usbser.sys) to the three
; serial interfaces of a Quectel EC20 / EC25 (USB 2C7C:0125), NMEA, AT and
; modem, so that they appear as COM ports on Windows 10/11 ARM64, in every
; USB network mode of the module. This package contains no
; driver binary: usbser.sys and usbser.inf ship with Windows.
;
; What is matched, and why not by hardware ID:
;   The EC20 renumbers its interfaces with the USB network mode
;   (AT+QCFG="usbnet"), so an interface number does not name a role. In
;   QMI and MBIM mode MI_00 is the DM port; in RNDIS mode MI_00 is the
;   RNDIS control interface (class E0/01/03), and a hardware-ID match on
;   USB\VID_2C7C&PID_0125&MI_00 outranks the in-box RNDIS driver's
;   class match (wceisvista.inf, USB\Class_E0&SubClass_01&Prot_03): on the
;   TaishanPi 3M (2026-11-26) the RNDIS function got usbser.sys and failed
;   to start. The rank of a match cannot depend on the class when the
;   match is by hardware ID, so this INF matches by a compatible ID that
;   carries the class instead:
;
;     USB\COMPAT_VID_2c7c&Class_ff&SubClass_00&Prot_00
;
;   Windows generates it for every interface of a Quectel (VID 2C7C)
;   composite device; seen on the board on the EC20's FF/00/00 interfaces.
;   In every usbnet mode, class FF/00/00 is exactly the NMEA, AT and modem
;   ports, and nothing else:
;
;            usbnet 0 QMI   usbnet 2 MBIM  usbnet 3 RNDIS
;   MI_00    DM     FF/FF/FF DM     FF/FF/FF RNDIS  E0/01/03 (+ data)
;   MI_01    NMEA   FF/00/00 NMEA   FF/00/00 (part of the RNDIS function)
;   MI_02    AT     FF/00/00 AT     FF/00/00 DM     FF/FF/FF
;   MI_03    modem  FF/00/00 modem  FF/00/00 NMEA   FF/00/00
;   MI_04    QMI    FF/FF/FF MBIM   02/0E/00 AT     FF/00/00
;   MI_05    audio  01       (MBIM data)     modem  FF/00/00
;   MI_06                    audio  01       audio  01
;
;   (README.md has this table with what was measured and what inferred.)
;   Not matched, on purpose:
;   - DM (FF/FF/FF). In QMI mode the QMI network function is FF/FF/FF too,
;     and the only thing that tells the two apart is the interface number,
;     which moves. Binding usbser.sys to the QMI function would take it
;     from any QMI driver installed later, so DM stays without a driver.
;     Nothing here uses DM.
;   - The network functions (QMI, MBIM 02/0E, RNDIS E0, ECM 02/06): they
;     keep the in-box class drivers (cxwmbclass, wceisvista) or none.
;   - USB audio (01): in-box usbaudio.
;   Side effect: the match names the vendor, not the product, so FF/00/00
;   interfaces of other Quectel USB modules get usbser.sys as well. On the
;   Qualcomm-based modules (EC2x, EG2x, EM05, EP06) those are the same kind
;   of NMEA/AT/modem ports.
;   All three ports get the same name; the scripts tell them apart by
;   order: the lowest-numbered FF/00/00 interface is NMEA, then AT, then
;   modem.
;
; The interface roles come from the Linux kernel: drivers/usb/serial/option.c
; (EC25/EC20 entries match by class: FF/FF/FF with two endpoints is DIAG,
; FF/00/00 the serial ports) and Quectel's "LTE&5G Linux USB Driver User
; Guide" V2.0, Table 2 and section 3.2.5 (network interfaces skipped).
;
; Why usbser.sys: Microsoft documents usbser.sys for CDC ACM devices
; (class 02, subclass 02), see "USB serial driver (Usbser.sys)":
; https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/drivers/usbcon/usb-driver-installation-based-on-compatible-ids
; These interfaces are vendor class (FF), so usbser.sys does not load on its
; own. They do implement the CDC ACM requests that matter for an AT port:
; Linux sends CDC SET_CONTROL_LINE_STATE to them (drivers/usb/serial/usb_wwan.c)
; and reads CDC SERIAL_STATE notifications from their interrupt endpoint
; (option_instat_callback in drivers/usb/serial/option.c). Whether usbser.sys
; accepts a single vendor-class interface with no CDC functional descriptors
; is NOT documented by Microsoft and has NOT been tested on hardware. If a
; port shows Code 10 after installing this package, install the WinUSB
; package (inf\winusb\ec20-winusb.inf) instead and use tools\ec20at.exe.
;
; How usbser.inf is reused: by Include= and Needs= of the sections of the
; inbox usbser.inf (UsbSerial_Install.NT, .NT.Hw and .NT.Services). That is
; how usbser.inf itself marks those sections ("for use with Include/Needs").
; The older pattern in Microsoft's documentation, Include=mdmcpq.inf with
; usbser as a lower filter, is not usable here: mdmcpq.inf is not present on
; the target Windows 11 23H2 ARM64 install.
;
; Unofficial. Not written, tested or endorsed by Quectel. MIT licence, see
; LICENSE in the repository.
;

[Version]
Signature   = "$WINDOWS NT$"
Class       = Ports
ClassGuid   = {4D36E978-E325-11CE-BFC1-08002BE10318}
Provider    = %Provider%
CatalogFile = ec20-ports.cat
DriverVer   = 11/26/2026,2.0.0.0
PnpLockdown = 1

[Manufacturer]
%Mfg% = Ec20Ports, NTarm64.10.0

[Ec20Ports.NTarm64.10.0]
%Ec20.Serial% = Ec20Port_Install, USB\COMPAT_VID_2c7c&Class_ff&SubClass_00&Prot_00

; Everything below is taken from the inbox usbser.inf by reference: the copy
; of usbser.sys, the Ports class registry values (PortSubClass and the
; MsPorts.dll property page) and the usbser service.
[Ec20Port_Install.NT]
Include = usbser.inf
Needs   = UsbSerial_Install.NT

[Ec20Port_Install.NT.HW]
Include = usbser.inf
Needs   = UsbSerial_Install.NT.Hw

[Ec20Port_Install.NT.Services]
Include = usbser.inf
Needs   = UsbSerial_Install.NT.Services

[Strings]
Provider    = "ec20-woa (unofficial)"
Mfg         = "Quectel EC2x, ec20-woa community INF"
Ec20.Serial = "Quectel EC2x serial port (usbser)"
